// The Ring 1 set update methods `add`/`remove` (value semantics: each returns a NEW set, kept sorted +
// de-duplicated). `add` of an existing member is a no-op copy; `remove` of an absent one likewise. An
// aliased snapshot taken before an update is never mutated by it. Sets are not indexed, so there is no
// `s[i] = v` sugar — these are methods only. Both backends agree.
// expect: stdout "{1, 2, 3, 5}"
// expect: stdout "true"
// expect: stdout "{1, 3, 5}"
// expect: stdout "{1, 2, 3, 5}"
// expect: exit 0
mut s = #{3, 1, 2};
s = s.add(5);
s = s.add(2);
seen = s;
echo s;
echo s.contains(5);
s = s.remove(2);
echo s;
echo seen;
